  <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
          <div class="qh-layout__grid">
        
        <div class="qh-layout__content">
          
  

          
  <div data-drupal-messages-fallback class="hidden"></div>

<article class="qh__prose node node--type-topic node--view-mode-full">

      <h1>Immunization</h1>
  
    
    <div class="layout layout--onecol">
    <div  class="layout__region layout__region--content">
      <div class="qh__prose block block-layout-builder block-field-blocknodetopicfield-page-content-components">
  
    
        

  

          

                          

          
  

<figure class="qh__media-item qh__media-img small right">
            <a href=/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/2025-07/vaccine-guide.pdf title="A Guide to Vaccines: Your Pathway to Lifelong Protection">
          <img srcset="/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_325x325/public/2025-07/guide-to-vaccines.png?itok=N33kLY-_ 247w, /sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_650x650/public/2025-07/guide-to-vaccines.png?itok=FejZ9JG2 494w, /sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_1300x1300/public/2025-07/guide-to-vaccines.png?itok=q0pzSFWy 607w" sizes="(min-width: 1290px) 1290px, 100vw" width="247" height="325" src="/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_325x325/public/2025-07/guide-to-vaccines.png?itok=N33kLY-_" alt="A Guide to Vaccines: Your Pathway to Lifelong Protection" loading="lazy" />


      </a>
      
  
  
      <figcaption>A Guide to Vaccines: Your Pathway to Lifelong Protection</figcaption>
  </figure>




  

  

            <p>Rhode Island's statewide vaccine policies are grounded in the best available science, data, and evidence-based public health practice. Vaccines are the best way to prevent serious illness and death from many <a href="https://health.ri.gov/diseases">infectious diseases</a>. They protect people by preparing their immune systems to recognize and fight <a href="https://health.ri.gov/diseases#summary-28711">vaccine preventable diseases</a>. They also protect communities by reducing the spread of illness.</p>
<p><a href="http://www.fda.gov/">The Food and Drug Administration</a> adheres to rigorous testing standards before licensing vaccines to ensure <a href="/immunization/about/safety/">vaccine safety</a>. Leaders in the fields of medicine and public health recommend vaccines for children, adolescents, and adults to prevent disease.</p>
<p>Learn more about the right vaccines for you and your family in RIDOH's <a href="https://health.ri.gov/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/2025-07/vaccine-guide.pdf">A Guide to Vaccines: Your Pathway to Lifelong Protection</a> (<a href="/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/2025-11/vaccine-guide-es.pdf">Spanish</a>).</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>

      

  

  

            <h2>What You Should Do</h2>
<h3>Parents</h3>
<p>Make sure your children are up to date on all the American Academy of Pediatrics' (AAP's) <a href="https://downloads.aap.org/AAP/PDF/AAP-Immunization-Schedule.pdf">recommended vaccinations for children and adolescents</a>. <a href="/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/2026-02/child-immunization-schedule-public.pdf">Rhode Island’s recommended childhood immunization schedule</a>&nbsp;mirrors the AAP recommendations. Ask your children’s pediatrician about vaccinations at each visit.</p>
<p>Children without doctors who are uninsured can be vaccinated at the <a href="/immunization/services/asthenis-public-health-hub">Asthenis Public Health Hub</a>. Older children can also be immunized through <a href="/programs/detail.php?pgm_id=1010">Vaccinate Before You Graduate</a>, a catch-up program run at Rhode Island middle and high schools.</p>
<p>Explore more <a href="/immunization/information/parents">immunization information for parents</a> and <a href="/immunization/information/pregnant-people">immunization information for pregnant people</a>.&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Adults</h3>
<p>Several factors determine what immunizations adults need. Speak with a healthcare professional or review the American Academy of Family Physicians' <a href="https://www.aafp.org/clinical-insights/immunizations-and-vaccines/immunizations-schedules-resources">recommended vaccinations for adults</a> to know what immunizations you need.</p>
<h3>Travelers</h3>
<p>If you are planning to travel outside of the United States, you can get recommended vaccines at <a href="https://health.ri.gov/for/travelers/">travel clinics</a>.</p>
<h3>People Who Will Be in Contact with Infants</h3>
<p>Adults and adolescents who will be around infants should be up to date on all vaccines. This can include parents, siblings, grandparents, babysitters, and other childcare providers. Infants are too young to be fully immunized, but you can help protect them from certain illnesses by only letting them come into contact with people who are vaccinated. Infants can get very sick from diseases such as <a href="/diseases/vaccinepreventable/?parm=12">pertussis</a>, <a href="/hepatitis-viral/hepatitis-b">hepatitis B</a>, <a href="/infectious-diseases/measles">measles</a>, <a href="/flu-influenza">flu</a>, <a href="/covid-19-coronavirus">COVID-19</a>, and <a href="/infectious-diseases/rsv">respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)</a>.&nbsp;</p>

      

    <div class="paragraph paragraph--type--embed paragraph--view-mode--default">
          
            <div class="clearfix text-formatted field field--name-field-embed-content field--type-text-long field--label-hidden field__item"><div id="vaccine-clinic"></div>
<br></div>
      
      </div>

  

  


                          

          
  

<figure class="qh__media-item qh__media-img medium right">
              <img srcset="/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_325x325/public/2025-04/small-state-big-deal-banner.png?itok=yTqT3Dd5 325w, /sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_650x650/public/2025-04/small-state-big-deal-banner.png?itok=cdqPb9BD 650w, /sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_1300x1300/public/2025-04/small-state-big-deal-banner.png?itok=NAkfL0mR 1200w" sizes="(min-width: 1290px) 1290px, 100vw" width="325" height="108" src="/sites/g/files/xkgbur1006/files/styles/max_325x325/public/2025-04/small-state-big-deal-banner.png?itok=yTqT3Dd5" alt="Small State Big Deal" loading="lazy" />


      
  
  
  </figure>




  

  

            <h2>Where You Can Find Vaccine Clinics and Programs</h2>
<p>You can find local vaccine clinics and programs in these places:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="/immunization/services/asthenis-public-health-hub"><strong>Asthenis Public Health Hub</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Offers a range of vaccinations for children and adults.</li>
<li><a href="https://health.ri.gov/programs/detail.php?pgm_id=1010"><strong>Vaccinate Before You Graduate</strong></a><strong>:</strong> A program designed to ensure middle and high school students are up-to-date on their vaccines.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.vaxassist.com/"><strong>VaxAssist</strong></a><strong>: </strong>Find COVID-19, flu, RSV, and pneumococcal pneumonia vaccines based on your location and vaccine type.</li>
</ul>

      

  

  

            <h3>Community Clinics</h3>
<p>2026-2027 vaccination clinics are held at schools in the late afternoon and evening and are open to the entire community. You can currently register for <a href="/flu-influenza">flu</a> vaccine and&nbsp;<a href="/covid-19-coronavirus">COVID-19</a> vaccine, which will be available at these clinics for people age 3 and older. <a href="https://www.thewellcomp.com/Registrations/chooseschool.aspx">Register for school and community clinic vaccinations at schoolflu.com</a>.&nbsp;</p>
<ul>
<li>It's safe to get both vaccines at the same visit</li>
<li>No insurance required</li>
<li>No out-of-pocket cost for vaccination</li>
<li>You can get the 2026-2027 COVID-19 vaccine even if you haven't gotten a COVID-19 vaccine in the past&nbsp;</li>
</ul>
<p>You must pre-register to guarantee an appointment. You can select any available timeslot as a placeholder. Walk-ins are accepted on a limited basis, as resources allow.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>

      

  

  

            <h2>What We Are Doing</h2>
<p>Rhode Island provides healthcare professionals with all recommended routine vaccines for children. Rhode Island also provides healthcare professionals with most recommended vaccines for adults. Although some healthcare professionals may charge patients small fees to give them vaccines, Rhode Island gives these vaccines to healthcare professionals free of charge.</p>
<p>The Rhode Island Department of Health also works with mass immunizers, schools, healthcare professionals, and many others to make vaccines available in the community.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>

      

  
